Root cause:
The bug was caused by an "off-by-one" error in the loop conditions used in the `gfs_bind` and `gfs_unbind` functions. The pre-decrement operation (`--i`) in the loop condition caused the zeroth instance to be skipped, leading to incorrect binding and unbinding of functionfs instances. This resulted in the first instance being processed only when there were multiple instances, thus failing to handle the zeroth instance properly.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
for (i = func_num; --i; )
```
Reason:
The pre-decrement operation (`--i`) causes the loop to skip the zeroth instance of the functionfs, as it decrements `i` before the first iteration. This means that when `i` is initialized to `func_num`, the loop starts from `func_num - 1`, effectively ignoring the instance at index 0.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
for (i = func_num; i--; )
```
Reason:
Changing the loop condition to `i--;` ensures that the loop starts from `func_num - 1` and includes the zeroth instance in the iterations. This allows all instances, including the zeroth, to be properly bound or unbound, thus fixing the "off-by-one" error.


